# tools/caravan_ladder_reach_visibility_witness.rish -- the widening cost of a
# fold is measured, never described.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/caravan_ladder_reach_visibility_witness.rish
#
# The lift arc weighs every family by two numbers: the fall it returns, and the
# declarations it would have to widen. The first has always come off a meter.
# The second came off a hand reading a diff, and on 20260822 it was measured for
# the first time and disagreed with the record on both families it named.
#
# `confirm` was written as widening exactly one declaration, because `read_count`
# stood private in one of the forty-six rungs. `read_count` is absent from that
# rung entirely -- it is `entrust.rye`, whose `confirm` waits on a different note
# through a different reader, so it was never a fold candidate and never a cost.
#
# `renew_path` and `ask_path` were each written as widening thirty-one, because
# the family stands private in all thirty-one rungs. A family's own visibility is
# not its widening cost. The cost is what its BODY reaches, and both bodies reach
# only symbols already public in every rung -- so the largest fall on the board
# carried a price of zero and was passed over four laps running (REDS %130).
#
# So the queue's second column is a measurement now. The scan hashes every body
# under a name, takes the largest identical set, drops the rungs standing below
# the fold line, reads the identifiers the shared body reaches unqualified, and
# reports how many of those each folding rung keeps private.
#
# The column split (`20260822.101058`). One `widens` number counted two unlike
# things. Reading the seven symbols behind the whole remaining queue against
# their own declaration lines found five to be plain module import bindings and
# two to be functions, so `widens` now carries `widens_fn`, `widens_import`, and
# `widens_const` beside it. Publishing an import binding re-exports a module the
# rung already names in its own head -- no behavior opened -- while publishing a
# function opens what the rung offers a caller. A count that sums across kinds
# has thrown away the mark at the moment it mattered, and the sum reads as
# authoritative precisely because it is a number. The design read is
# `active-designing/20260822-101058_what-a-widening-costs-a-rungs-door.md`.
#
# Both refusals are proven on metal by generated controls -- a name no rung
# declares, and a name whose every body differs -- since a scan finding nothing
# must never answer ok about its own blindness (REDS %97).

say "reach-visibility: Language EN -- what a fold must widen is read off the body, never off the family's own door."
say "reach-visibility: Style Radiant -- the measurement leads, and the queue answers to it."
say "reach-visibility: Lens TAME -- the shape is measured, the fold line is honored, and both blindnesses refuse by name."

let red = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "prove-red"]
say red.out
assert red.out contains "RED_no_family_refused_rather_than_ok" else "reach-visibility: a name no rung declares answered ok -- a scan must never bless its own blindness"
assert red.out contains "RED_no_identical_refused_rather_than_ok" else "reach-visibility: a name whose every body differs answered ok -- nothing folds there"
assert red.out contains "RED_capitalized_type_priced_rather_than_unseen" else "reach-visibility: a private type the shared body names priced at zero -- a meter that reads only lowercase names cannot see a struct (REDS %140)"

# The family this lap lifted. Sixty of its bodies are stubs now, and seven stay
# home below the fold line, since ladder_checks.rye imports each of them and a
# lift would have the harness reach back through a rung it already reaches for.
# It is the first lift of the arc to take no rung at all: the body reaches only
# `system` and `read`, both shared helper modules the harness imports itself, so
# it paid none of the hundred and twenty widenings the meter priced. The scan
# reads the folded shape back -- no long body left to lift, so the widening
# column now describes the stubs.
let folded = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "load_one"]
say folded.out
assert folded.out contains "REACH_OK family=load_one folding=60 widens=120 widens_fn=0 widens_import=120 widens_const=0 stub=3 fall=0" else "reach-visibility: want load_one standing folded, with nothing left to lift"
assert folded.ok else "reach-visibility: the lifted family must still read cleanly"

# The row that costs the most, now that the column says what it means. Every
# symbol elder_waits reaches privately is a function -- `waits`, held private in
# forty-three rungs -- so folding it opens what those rungs offer a caller. This
# is the shape a design decision belongs to, and it is asserted here so the
# queue can never quietly re-sort it back to the front on a fall alone. It is the
# only one of the five asserted here whose openings are functions.
let head = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "elder_waits"]
say head.out
assert head.out contains "REACH_FAMILY family=elder_waits declared=53 identical=43 lines=15" else "reach-visibility: want elder_waits standing as fifty-three declarations holding forty-three identical bodies at fifteen lines"
assert head.out contains "REACH_OK family=elder_waits folding=43 widens=43 widens_fn=43 widens_import=0 widens_const=0 stub=3 fall=504" else "reach-visibility: want the queue head returning five hundred and four for forty-three openings"
assert head.ok else "reach-visibility: the queue head must read cleanly"

# read_own_line folded this lap, so the same scan run against it now reads a
# fall of zero -- which is how this meter tells the bench a door is already
# through. Its whole widening was import bindings -- `capabilities`, `carry`,
# and `cycle`, every one of them a shared helper module the harness already
# imports -- so the lift paid none of the hundred and twenty-six. Read by the
# old single column it stood among the most expensive rows remaining; read by
# kind it was free of the only cost that was ever real, and the fold proved it.
let sibling = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "read_own_line"]
say sibling.out
assert sibling.out contains "REACH_OK family=read_own_line folding=42 widens=126 widens_fn=0 widens_import=126 widens_const=0 stub=7 fall=0" else "reach-visibility: want read_own_line reading a fall of zero, its family already folded"
assert sibling.ok else "reach-visibility: the folded family must read cleanly"

# Four folded readings kept as regression pins -- the four most recent lifts of
# this arc, slid forward one family per lap. The arc has folded far more than
# four and this witness cannot pin them all, so it pins the newest window and
# lets the carry meter's own ceiling hold the rest: a body that came back would
# raise the carry, and the ceiling only ever comes down.

# The widest family the arc has lifted -- forty-three rungs at fourteen lines,
# all forty-three folding -- whose forty-three priced widenings were forty-three
# import bindings of `system`, a module this harness already imports at its own
# head. After the lift each rung holds a three-line stub, so the column reads
# eighty-six import bindings and the fall reads zero.
let free_head = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "door_admits"]
say free_head.out
assert free_head.out contains "REACH_OK family=door_admits folding=43 widens=86 widens_fn=0 widens_import=86 widens_const=0 stub=3 fall=0" else "reach-visibility: want door_admits reading a fall of zero, its family already folded"

# The cleanest row the arc has taken -- twenty-one rungs widening nothing of any
# kind, its rung declaration staying private through the lift, so a body moved
# and nothing at all was published.
let next_head = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "finding_path"]
say next_head.out
assert next_head.out contains "REACH_OK family=finding_path folding=21 widens=21 widens_fn=0 widens_import=21 widens_const=0 stub=3 fall=0" else "reach-visibility: want finding_path reading a fall of zero, its family already folded"

# The row the lap before this one folded. Forty rungs wrote it identically at
# twelve lines and every one of them folded, since this harness imports none of
# the forty. It takes the rung for five names -- the phase bound, the phase
# reader, the note writer, the domain name, and the error set -- and all five
# stood public already, so the lift opened nothing at all. Read after the fold
# it prices the forty stubs' own private import of this harness, which is what
# every folded family reads.
let clean_row = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "lost_note"]
say clean_row.out
assert clean_row.out contains "REACH_OK family=lost_note folding=40 widens=40 widens_fn=0 widens_import=40 widens_const=0 stub=3 fall=0" else "reach-visibility: want lost_note reading a fall of zero, its family already folded"

# The family this lap folded, and the first lift of this arc to pay a widening
# rather than find one already open. Thirty-seven rungs wrote it identically at
# sixteen lines and every one folded. The meter priced seventy-four openings and
# the lift paid thirty-seven of them: the import bindings all bind the outcome
# module, which this harness imports at its own head and reaches directly, while
# the struct openings publish a `Finished` -- one reaped dependent held until the
# record drains -- because a type standing in a published signature must stand
# public beside it. Read after the fold it prices the thirty-seven stubs' own
# private import of this harness, and the fall reads zero.
let this_lap = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "judge_outcome"]
say this_lap.out
assert this_lap.out contains "REACH_OK family=judge_outcome folding=37 widens=37 widens_fn=0 widens_import=37 widens_const=0 stub=3 fall=0" else "reach-visibility: want judge_outcome reading a fall of zero, its family already folded"
assert this_lap.ok else "reach-visibility: this lap's folded family must read cleanly"

# The row that leads the queue now. sibling_waits folds forty-three rungs of
# thirteen lines and buys its extra body with function openings rather than with
# import bindings -- forty-three of its widenings publish what a rung offers a
# caller, which is a wider door than an import binding and is the whole of what a
# lap weighing this row must weigh.
let free_next = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "sibling_waits"]
say free_next.out
assert free_next.out contains "REACH_FAMILY family=sibling_waits declared=55 identical=43 lines=13" else "reach-visibility: want sibling_waits standing as fifty-five declarations holding forty-three identical bodies at thirteen lines"
assert free_next.out contains "REACH_OK family=sibling_waits folding=43 widens=86 widens_fn=43 widens_import=43 widens_const=0 stub=3 fall=420" else "reach-visibility: want sibling_waits priced at eighty-six, half of them function openings"
assert free_next.ok else "reach-visibility: the next head must read cleanly"

# The row standing behind it, and the row that proves the corrected fall formula
# on the living ladder. may_pass stands with three rungs below its fold line, so
# two of them go on carrying between themselves after a lift, and its fall came
# down from five hundred and seventy-four to five hundred and thirty-six when the
# formula learned to count the third population (REDS %136).
let third = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "may_pass"]
say third.out
assert third.out contains "REACH_FAMILY family=may_pass declared=58 identical=51 lines=19" else "reach-visibility: want may_pass standing as fifty-eight declarations holding fifty-one identical bodies at nineteen lines"
assert third.out contains "REACH_OK family=may_pass folding=48 widens=101 widens_fn=48 widens_import=48 widens_const=5 stub=8 fall=536" else "reach-visibility: want may_pass carrying the corrected fall, three rungs standing below its fold line"
assert third.ok else "reach-visibility: the row behind the head must read cleanly"

# The fourth, and the largest fall the queue holds. choose_pass folds forty-three
# rungs of twenty-two lines for six hundred and thirty, and asks the widest price
# of any row standing: eighty-six function openings beside forty-three import
# bindings. A number a meter recites and never asserts is a claim rather than a
# measurement (REDS %110), so every family the GREEN line names stands asserted
# above it.
let fourth = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "choose_pass"]
say fourth.out
assert fourth.out contains "REACH_FAMILY family=choose_pass declared=59 identical=43 lines=22" else "reach-visibility: want choose_pass standing as fifty-nine declarations holding forty-three identical bodies at twenty-two lines"
assert fourth.out contains "REACH_OK family=choose_pass folding=43 widens=129 widens_fn=86 widens_import=43 widens_const=0 stub=7 fall=630" else "reach-visibility: want choose_pass priced at one hundred and twenty-nine, two thirds of them function openings"
assert fourth.ok else "reach-visibility: the fourth family must read cleanly"

# The fold line priced, rather than described (REDS %137). A rung the harness
# imports stands below the fold line from that moment on and can never fold a
# body again, so a harness-level import is a one-way door -- and this lap's
# first cut called that door expensive in a GREEN line without ever measuring
# it, which is REDS %130's lantern firing a second time. Measured, the whole
# fold line forecloses 235 body lines out of 62,303, and the two rungs this lap
# declined to sink hold three lines each. The cost was real and small.
#
# The finding is the spread rather than the total. A rung the harness imports
# tends to be a shared helper, and a shared helper writes few duplicated bodies
# -- while an ordinary rung like farewell.rye holds 3,767. Three orders of
# magnitude between them is why "always keep the rung parameter" is as wrong as
# "always import," and why the door is priced before it is opened.
let sink = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh" "sink"]
say sink.out
assert sink.out contains "SINK_OK total=62303 below_fold_line=235 above_fold_line=62068" else "reach-visibility: want the fold line's whole foreclosure measured against the foldable corpus"
assert sink.out contains "SINK_ABOVE rung=queue.rye holds=3" else "reach-visibility: want the queue store's holding read off disk rather than described"
assert sink.out contains "SINK_ABOVE rung=serve.rye holds=3" else "reach-visibility: want the serve module's holding read off disk rather than described"
assert sink.out contains "SINK_ABOVE rung=farewell.rye holds=3767" else "reach-visibility: want the widest holding above the line, so the spread is a measurement rather than a feeling"
assert sink.out contains "SINK_BELOW rung=cohort.rye holds=90" else "reach-visibility: want the largest holding already below the fold line named"
assert sink.ok else "reach-visibility: the sink meter must read cleanly"

# The kind rides on every private symbol line too, so a reader scanning the
# detail sees what each opening actually is rather than only that it is one.
assert sibling.out contains "REACH_PRIVATE symbol=capabilities kind=import" else "reach-visibility: want each private symbol naming its kind beside it"
assert head.out contains "REACH_PRIVATE symbol=waits kind=fn" else "reach-visibility: want a private function named as a function"

say "GREEN: reach-visibility -- the widening cost of a fold is read off the body it lifts, the fold line is honored, the cost is counted by kind rather than summed across kinds, and the fall counts every population left standing rather than two of the three. This lap judge_outcome folded: thirty-seven rungs wrote it identically and every one of them folded, since this harness imports none of the thirty-seven and no rung of the family stood below the fold line. It is the first lift of this arc to pay a widening rather than find one already open. The meter priced the family in two even halves and the lift paid one of them: the import bindings all bind the outcome module, which this harness imports at its own head and reaches directly, while the struct openings publish a Finished -- one reaped dependent held until the record drains -- because a type standing in a published signature must stand public beside it, and every one of the thirty-seven already published a judge_outcome taking one. sibling_waits leads the queue now, buying its extra body with function openings, which publish what a rung offers a caller; may_pass stands behind it carrying the corrected fall that three rungs below its fold line had hidden; and choose_pass holds the largest fall of any row standing and asks the widest price for it. A count that sums across kinds throws the mark away at the moment it matters, and a formula that names two populations where three stand overprices every fold that has more than one rung standing home. The readings above are the most recent lifts and next heads, slid forward one family per lap: the arc has folded far more than these and this witness pins the newest window, letting the carry ceiling hold the rest, since a body that came back would raise the carry and the ceiling only ever comes down. And the fold line itself is priced rather than described: importing a rung into the harness sinks it below the line forever, forfeiting the 235 body lines the whole line already holds against a foldable corpus of 62,303 -- and 3,767 for the widest rung still above it. Three orders of magnitude between them is why the door is measured before it is opened (REDS %137)."
